1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Hawaiians saw — four star
lines home.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
1976: a double canoe left Hawaiʻi with
no compass, no sextant, no map.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Its name, Hōkūleʻa, means “star of
gladness”: Arcturus.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
From Satawal, Micronesia. Hawaiʻi’s own
deep-sea wayfinding was long lost.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
His student Nainoa Thompson split the
sky into four north–south star lines.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Not an ancient star map — a modern
navigator’s notes for memorising the
sky.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
From the Southern Cross, past Hōkūleʻa,
up to Hōkūpaʻa: the North Star.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Winter: Makaliʻi’s bailer. Spring: the
backbone. Summer: the chief’s fishing
line. Autumn: Kawelo’s kite.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Sail north, and Hōkūleʻa climbs higher
every night.

10
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
From Capella to Sirius, it scoops up
Orion and the Pleiades as it rises.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Each November, Makaliʻi — the Pleiades —
rises at sunset. Four months for the
harvest god Lono begin.

12
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Around 8 p.m., the Pleiades stand high
in the south.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Navajo: star stories told only in
winter.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

